WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.126
There's a genre of sci-fi

00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:04.754
that gives us a glimpse into the future
that's totally different.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:07.841
In fact,
the polar opposite of the dystopia

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:12.137
that we see in Cyberpunk,
and it's called SolarPunk.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:17.600
It's idyllic,
it's sustainable, it's communal,

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.769
and it's

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:22.063
a yogurt ad.

00:00:22.063 --> 00:00:24.524
In fact, unlike Cyberpunk,

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:28.987
there's not a whole lot of examples
of good solar punk in mass media.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:34.034
And the best visual examples
seem to be kind of missing the point.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:37.704
When we analyzed solar punk
from an architectural and urban planning

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:43.043
point of view, we found out just how many
parallels there were to our reality.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:48.006
So solar punk, could this be our future?

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:50.383
A portion of this video sponsored by HP

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:55.638
and Intel.

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:56.681
This video

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:59.893
is probably one of the best depictions
of solar punk.

00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:04.814
It's a future where we merge
fusion technology with eco centric values

00:01:05.023 --> 00:01:07.776
and we thrive in harmony with nature.

00:01:07.776 --> 00:01:11.446
But the more we learned
about the movement, we realize

00:01:11.529 --> 00:01:14.324
the existence of this video is

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:17.327
actually the antithesis of solar punk.

00:01:17.368 --> 00:01:21.498
The punk in both solar and cyberpunk
refers to rebellion.

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:25.210
Cyberpunk is against an oppressive techno
dystopia,

00:01:25.293 --> 00:01:29.380
and solar punk is against consumerism
and capitalism, which is leading

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.965
to the destruction of the planet.

00:01:30.965 --> 00:01:34.427
The solar is both literal
and metaphorical.

00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:38.556
Literally, it represents solar
and other renewable energies.

00:01:38.556 --> 00:01:41.935
And metaphorically
it's talking about this beacon of light

00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:47.482
in a dark cyberpunk world
run by corporate interests.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:49.150
So an ad

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:54.447
using these beautiful solar punk esthetics
and using this utopian vision

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:59.953
to convince people to buy their products
seems very anti-social or punk.

00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:03.373
And unlike cyberpunk,
which is a huge part of pop culture,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:08.253
the real solar punk
seems to only exist in classify

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:12.173
novels or in a really small
niche section of the Internet.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:17.387
There are films and video games out there
that fit the solar punk esthetic,

00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:21.057
but they don't quite fit
the true definition of solar punk

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:25.395
when it comes to the social
or cultural aspects socially.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:28.773
It emphasizes unity,
a shared responsibility

00:02:28.898 --> 00:02:33.153
and harmony
between both nature and fellow humans.

00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:35.947
And the combination of these words

00:02:35.947 --> 00:02:40.368
kind of immediately
make you think of a cult.

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:45.248
And why is it

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.292
maybe it's Hollywood

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:52.422
who's done a really good job of
glamorizing this idea of the sexy, rugged

00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:58.094
rebel with a six pack on a motorbike
navigating this dark dystopian world?

00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:04.475
I mean, there is nothing really sexy
about Ryan Gosling in a far market.

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:07.270
Okay, that's still pretty hot.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.439
Let's look at Wakanda from Black Panther.

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:13.568
It's probably one of the few iconic
solar powered visions from Hollywood.

00:03:13.735 --> 00:03:16.988
It's the most technologically advanced
country in the world.

00:03:17.113 --> 00:03:21.826
But instead of a city like this, it's
surrounded by sweeping valleys

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:22.744
and mountains.

00:03:22.744 --> 00:03:25.872
Technically,
Wakanda doesn't qualify as solar power

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:30.919
because it's a matriarchal society
and it's not really culturally diverse.

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:36.466
But there is a beautiful integration
of technology into the city's fabric,

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:39.552
and at the same time,
the entire society is grounded

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.555
in traditions
that's passed down for generations.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.309
But I think the most solar punk elements
Wakanda actually came

00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:50.772
from the location of the Golden City
and this abrupt transition

00:03:50.772 --> 00:03:54.776
between the city
and the surrounding landscape.

00:03:54.817 --> 00:03:57.237
If you look at any North American city,

00:03:57.237 --> 00:04:00.865
the periphery is surrounded
by highways and suburbs.

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:04.494
Something like 52% of Americans
live in suburbs.

00:04:04.535 --> 00:04:08.623
Suburbs emerged partly
because cities were polluted and dangerous

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:10.583
and weren't really nice to live in.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:16.256
So in the fifties and sixties, as soon as
we had the sudden boom in car ownership,

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:20.176
people realized, Hey,
I could live in bigger houses

00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:25.807
with green space for less money
if I just moved out of the city.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:29.560
But studies show that suburban dwellers
emit up to four times

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.563
the amount of emission
the people living in urban areas

00:04:32.647 --> 00:04:35.984
because you're dependent on cars,
there's longer commutes

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:39.195
and you're sharing less
and less resources.

00:04:39.195 --> 00:04:41.948
And the creation of a new suburb

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:45.910
often consumes
natural habitats and agricultural lands.

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:50.623
Suburbs also lead to segregated
neighborhoods with very little cultural

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:55.461
or social diversity, which can contribute
to a bigger socioeconomic divide.

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:58.840
Rather than trying to address
the problems in the city.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.468
We simply moved our population
to the outskirts.

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:07.223
And to this day, in many parts of North
America, the suburbs keep growing.

00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:12.270
It's ironic because the idea of a tight
knit community living in open space

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:16.274
with greenery, there is a lot of parallels
to solar Park ideals.

00:05:16.357 --> 00:05:19.527
In fact,
both the design of suburbs and solar punk

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:28.870
were inspired
by the same idea The Garden City movement.

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:29.954
If you want to talk about

00:05:29.954 --> 00:05:35.501
real polluted and dirty cities,
we have to look at London, England.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:40.089
During the late 19th century,
industrialization completely transformed

00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:45.428
London into one of the most slum,
disgusting, unhealthy places to live in.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:49.932
Sanitation was virtually non-existent,
and the factories,

00:05:49.932 --> 00:05:52.226
they just dumped everything
into the river,

00:05:52.226 --> 00:05:55.396
which is also
where they got their drinking water

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.442
because the industrial revolution
was completely unprecedented

00:05:59.609 --> 00:06:03.529
and cities just weren't equipped to handle
the kind of influx of people

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:04.489
and factories.

00:06:04.489 --> 00:06:07.158
This is when Ebenezer Howard,
an urban planner

00:06:07.158 --> 00:06:10.745
from London, came up
with this utopian concept for cities

00:06:10.912 --> 00:06:14.999
living in nature
and called them Garden Cities of Tomorrow.

00:06:15.083 --> 00:06:19.879
A group of slum less smokeless
cities, each city

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:24.634
or community would have a central district
that was dedicated to industry and work.

00:06:24.675 --> 00:06:27.845
From there
you have concentric rings of housing,

00:06:27.845 --> 00:06:31.474
gardens, agriculture,
leisure space and civic institutions.

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:34.435
These circles
were just posed to be diagrammatic.

00:06:34.435 --> 00:06:39.524
But I think what's interesting
is that these circular green belts

00:06:39.607 --> 00:06:44.654
were a boundary and you could limit
the growth of the population by design.

00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:45.822
And when you limit growth,

00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:49.700
you can also control the density, the cost
and the quality of life.

00:06:49.784 --> 00:06:52.328
He even suggested roughly sizes.

00:06:52.328 --> 00:06:55.957
Each city would be roughly 6000 acres,
with anywhere

00:06:55.957 --> 00:07:00.628
between 32000 to 58000 people
and the city ring

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:04.882
would occupy one six of that space,
leaving the rest to green space.

00:07:05.049 --> 00:07:10.388
These experimental garden cities started
to pop up all over America and the U.K.,

00:07:10.596 --> 00:07:14.350
but very few actually became successful
because while

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.644
it had the look of a Garden city,

00:07:16.644 --> 00:07:21.232
it lacked a lot of the other
characteristics like an industry, food

00:07:21.232 --> 00:07:25.987
production, civic institutions, meaning
people would still need to take their cars

00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:34.120
and drive to get to these places,
which in turn created suburbs.

