00:08:59.747 --> 00:09:02.291
This is Almir in Flevoland Netherlands,

00:09:02.291 --> 00:09:05.169
which was also inspired
by the Garden City movement

00:09:05.169 --> 00:09:08.506
and according to an article in Blue
Labyrinth, could be considered

00:09:08.506 --> 00:09:11.467
the very first real world solar punk city.

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:15.221
All mirror is only possible
thanks to modern engineering.

00:09:15.304 --> 00:09:19.392
They literally brought a submerged
piece of land back from the sea.

00:09:19.642 --> 00:09:21.227
The whole reason behind

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:25.356
creating all mirror is to prevent
the growing population of Amsterdam

00:09:25.356 --> 00:09:30.361
and the Randstad area from expanding
into the designated green areas.

00:09:30.361 --> 00:09:34.615
And like the Garden City diagram,
it's a number of semi separate nuclei,

00:09:34.657 --> 00:09:37.743
each with its own neighborhoods,
facilities and identity

00:09:37.785 --> 00:09:41.122
connected through a shared infrastructure
and a city center.

00:09:41.163 --> 00:09:46.127
Different neighborhoods have distinct
identities, from colorful Regenboogbuurt

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:51.757
to the diverse Homeruskwartier
with over 1000 self-built homes.

00:09:51.841 --> 00:09:55.720
The districts are separated
by water and parks and woodlands

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.347
or agricultural land
where they produce food.

00:09:58.347 --> 00:10:02.310
And apparently Almir has over 100%
more green space

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.980
per inhabitant than any other Dutch city
of a similar size.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:09.900
It's the newest city in the Netherlands,
and yet it's considered

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:12.028
the most successful new town.

00:10:12.028 --> 00:10:14.989
It's only a few decades old,
but it's become Netherland's

00:10:14.989 --> 00:10:17.658
eighth most populated city.

00:10:17.742 --> 00:10:18.909
In a lot of ways.

00:10:18.909 --> 00:10:22.830
Almir sounds like the perfect
implementation of Garden City

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:26.584
and solar punk ideals, but

00:10:26.667 --> 00:10:28.336
when you look closely,

00:10:28.336 --> 00:10:32.006
you start to notice
behind the sustainable facade.

00:10:32.214 --> 00:10:36.802
It's also subject to the same
economic realities as any other city.

00:10:36.844 --> 00:10:40.514
Because to build a successful city,
for it to scale from a village

00:10:40.723 --> 00:10:44.935
to a city of thousands of people,
you need capital.

00:10:44.935 --> 00:10:48.856
Capital for infrastructure
or for governments, for administration.

00:10:48.939 --> 00:10:53.152
The locally grown food might not be enough
to sustain this larger

00:10:53.152 --> 00:10:56.781
population, so maybe you have to transport
food from other places.

00:10:56.906 --> 00:10:58.491
With a bigger population,

00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:02.244
there's probably going to be more
waste and pollution, even if it's designed

00:11:02.244 --> 00:11:06.707
with sustainable principles,
which again requires more money to manage.

00:11:06.874 --> 00:11:12.088
So most cities are designed
and made possible within this capitalistic

00:11:12.088 --> 00:11:16.676
framework zoning regulations
and even the layout of a city grid

00:11:16.717 --> 00:11:20.971
allows us to expand more efficiently
compared to a more organic

00:11:21.138 --> 00:11:24.183
mixed use layout, which is harder to plan.

00:11:24.225 --> 00:11:27.186
And it's just hard to please everyone.

00:11:27.311 --> 00:11:32.692
The scale is usually way beyond human
scale because in order to maximize profit,

00:11:32.858 --> 00:11:35.736
you need to maximize the size
of your building,

00:11:35.736 --> 00:11:39.615
often at the expense of green spaces
or community spaces.

00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:43.953
Many public spaces
are actually privatized,

00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:46.247
which can limit access to certain people.

00:11:46.247 --> 00:11:49.709
Even actual public spaces like Times
Square

00:11:49.792 --> 00:11:53.379
can be taken over by private interests
very quickly through marketing.

00:11:53.462 --> 00:11:56.716
Even things like transportation
networks are designed to support

00:11:56.716 --> 00:12:00.469
areas with high economic activity
or potential for growth.

00:12:00.636 --> 00:12:05.391
And of course, the thing that drives
everything is land value.

00:12:05.474 --> 00:12:07.059
So you have one party

00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:11.021
whose only interest is driving up
the value of their properties.

00:12:11.105 --> 00:12:14.024
But that's also where people live.

00:12:14.024 --> 00:12:17.069
So there's kind of an inherent conflict
there.

00:12:17.236 --> 00:12:21.449
Essentially, cities have very little to do
with the collective good

00:12:21.574 --> 00:12:27.037
that solar pump talks about and a lot
to do with individual or corporate wealth.

00:12:27.204 --> 00:12:32.877
And here we are
again, the foundations of cyberpunk.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.464
So what if instead of trying to force fit
solar

00:12:36.464 --> 00:12:45.055
punk into our existing idea of cities,
we start from the ground up.

00:12:45.097 --> 00:12:49.226
This is our
the hewison again in the Netherlands.

00:12:49.393 --> 00:12:52.480
It's a commune of 70 people
who organize themselves

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:56.817
into a social critique, meaning
they make decisions

00:12:56.817 --> 00:13:00.738
through discussions and team consensus
rather than voting.

00:13:00.988 --> 00:13:04.617
They also grow their own food,
they share responsibilities,

00:13:04.617 --> 00:13:09.163
and they even build their own homes
using mostly recycled, reused

00:13:09.163 --> 00:13:13.459
and locally sourced building materials
like tires filled with rammed Earth,

00:13:13.584 --> 00:13:19.173
70% of their electricity and 100% of
their heating is generated on site

00:13:19.173 --> 00:13:22.635
and they minimize energy intake
with passive strategies.

00:13:22.635 --> 00:13:26.055
So from a social
and organizational perspective,

00:13:26.305 --> 00:13:28.599
the city is pretty solar punk.

00:13:28.599 --> 00:13:31.602
And it's not like there's
a size requirement for solar punk.

00:13:31.602 --> 00:13:35.523
And it could definitely be a low density
settlement like this article

00:13:35.523 --> 00:13:39.902
is, and grew out of the Earthship movement
from the late 20th century.

00:13:39.985 --> 00:13:42.988
The earliest community
was built in New Mexico,

00:13:43.155 --> 00:13:46.867
and it spread to small pockets
of communities around the world.

00:13:47.117 --> 00:13:50.955
And since they're built by
the local communities with local materials

00:13:50.955 --> 00:13:56.168
and site specific strategies, there's
a pretty big diversity in the designs.

00:13:56.168 --> 00:13:59.004
But that just might be the problem.

00:13:59.004 --> 00:14:07.930
Okay, when you think cyberpunk, what's
the first thing that comes to your mind?

00:14:08.013 --> 00:14:09.014
Brutalism has

00:14:09.014 --> 00:14:12.226
always been a pretty polarizing
architectural style.

00:14:12.268 --> 00:14:16.897
A lot of people
absolutely hate these concrete monsters,

00:14:16.981 --> 00:14:21.861
but it turns out when you put them in
the context of a bleak dystopian future,

00:14:21.944 --> 00:14:25.656
they actually have a certain
kind of allure, so much

00:14:25.656 --> 00:14:31.620
so that they become kind
of emblematic of cyberpunk.

00:14:31.704 --> 00:14:34.456
And this lack of a common
architectural language.

00:14:34.456 --> 00:14:40.296
And solar punk might seem trivial,
but architecture can often serve

00:14:40.296 --> 00:14:44.550
as the glue that binds social movements
or a cultural ethos.

00:14:44.592 --> 00:14:49.722
Whereas Mies van der Rohe said, it is the
will of an epoch translated into space.

00:14:49.722 --> 00:14:55.769
For example, the powerhouse movement
was born as a response to World War One.

00:14:55.853 --> 00:14:58.856
It symbolized
a vision of a new modern Europe.

00:14:58.981 --> 00:15:01.859
It emphasized
functionality and simplicity,

00:15:01.859 --> 00:15:04.778
and it said no to unnecessary ornaments.

00:15:04.778 --> 00:15:08.157
It was so culturally transformative
that the principle extended

00:15:08.157 --> 00:15:12.161
beyond architecture,
from art to furniture and textiles.

00:15:12.411 --> 00:15:15.414
And I think it really brought people
together.

00:15:15.497 --> 00:15:19.418
Solar punk right now
has a common esthetic,

00:15:19.501 --> 00:15:25.132
but it's not really rooted in
anything solid that our culture can grasp.

00:15:25.299 --> 00:15:33.307
So it's hard for us to imagine
it really existing in our reality.

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:36.602
In the last few
years, we've made some pretty big leaps

00:15:36.602 --> 00:15:40.397
in energy
storage and electric transportation.

00:15:40.481 --> 00:15:45.945
We have net zero buildings and also pretty
efficient systems for food production.

00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:50.532
But if we are aiming for a true
solar punk society

00:15:50.616 --> 00:15:54.912
and not the pretty solar punk renderings
that we see on Pinterest,

00:15:54.912 --> 00:15:59.124
the society outlined by these die
hard solar punk fans.

00:15:59.208 --> 00:16:03.128
Then there is a lot of economic,
structural,

00:16:03.128 --> 00:16:06.382
social and architectural challenges
that we'd have to overcome.

00:16:06.423 --> 00:16:10.594
But maybe it's just this kind of thinking.

00:16:10.636 --> 00:16:12.221
That's the problem.

00:16:12.221 --> 00:16:16.266
We've looked at a lot of cyberpunk
themes on this channel, and

00:16:16.266 --> 00:16:21.730
I think it's tapping into this collective
pessimism that a lot of us share.

00:16:21.939 --> 00:16:22.481
Okay.

00:16:22.481 --> 00:16:25.693
I'm not saying that there aren't
a lot of indications

00:16:25.693 --> 00:16:28.320
that we're headed towards
this dystopian future.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:33.909
There are, but we are kind of conditioned
to expect the worst.

00:16:34.076 --> 00:16:37.037
Neuroscientific research into defensive
pessimism

00:16:37.037 --> 00:16:40.332
tells us that pessimism
can actually reduce anxiety,

00:16:40.416 --> 00:16:44.545
especially when the outcome still
has the possibility to be prevented.

00:16:44.712 --> 00:16:49.883
And this is partly because the brain
is predisposed to react more quickly

00:16:49.883 --> 00:16:53.345
and readily to negative
stimuli compared to positive ones.

00:16:53.387 --> 00:16:56.473
I think one of the reasons
cyberpunk wins out

00:16:56.515 --> 00:17:01.603
is because being a pessimist is easier.

00:17:01.687 --> 00:17:05.274
I remember taking this class
on sustainability in university

00:17:05.357 --> 00:17:09.236
and just being completely
and totally depressed

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:11.989
because it just seemed like wherever
humans

00:17:11.989 --> 00:17:15.993
go, ecological devastation
inevitably follows.

00:17:16.118 --> 00:17:18.871
And this is the common rhetoric.

00:17:18.871 --> 00:17:23.500
And as we get older, I think we start
to believe in this idea more and more.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:26.378
We start believing
it's always been this way.

00:17:26.378 --> 00:17:29.506
So it's probably going to be this way
in the future.

00:17:29.631 --> 00:17:35.554
The thing that I like the most about solar
punk is the complete blatant rejection

00:17:35.554 --> 00:17:38.599
of this idea that humans can exist

00:17:38.599 --> 00:17:41.602
and thrive with nature and technology

00:17:41.769 --> 00:17:46.398
and that a beautiful, glowing,
sustainable future is possible.

00:17:46.482 --> 00:17:50.694
It's solution oriented
rather than dwelling on the problems.

00:17:50.778 --> 00:18:02.539
It's the harder thing to do,
and maybe that's what makes it worth it.

00:18:02.623 --> 00:18:03.749
Thanks for watching, guys.

00:18:03.749 --> 00:18:05.459
Which team are you on?

00:18:05.459 --> 00:18:08.170
Cyberpunk or solar punk?

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:10.089
Let me know in the comments below.

00:18:10.089 --> 00:18:12.883
Also,
we have a newsletter and a Discord server.

00:18:12.883 --> 00:18:14.676
We'll leave the links in the description.

00:18:14.676 --> 00:18:17.012
And with that,
I'll see you guys in the next video.

